    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "01238657_a4bdc783",
        "filename": "common/console_output.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1330845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-07-19T15:46:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Apologies for hopping on this late...\n\nIsn\u0027t it pretty standard to have something like this:\n\n    #define TIMESTAMP_FORMAT \"%.6lu\"\n\nThen use like so:\n\n    CPRINTF(\"[\" TIMESTAMP_FORMAT \" ...\", get_time().val, ...)\n\nTIMESTAMP_FORMAT can then be redefined as needed...\n\n%p is not a great idea, as the only reason the compile-time format checker works is that the following chars are assumed to be part of the output. See https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id\u003d47781#c18 for further discussion of this.\n\nFurther, if we ever wish to change to glibc (or any other standard implementation of) printf, the TIMESTAMP_FORMAT would work with that as well.",
      "parentUuid": "2ac03883_0d672c96",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "0ab02441dc7eb1263fed95935d63bce7ad92e35a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "95fff087_3af8e2ad",
        "filename": "common/console_output.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1260470
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-07-19T17:18:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Daisuke,\nSure, I can make a WIP CL as well, but for folks using %T explicitly instead of this:\nCPRINTF(\"[\");\nCPRINT_TIMESTAMP();\nCPRINTF(\" This is my print]\\n\");\n\nIt would look like this:\nCPRINTF(\"[%pT This is my print]\\n\", NULL);\n\nJack,\nI don\u0027t see a problem with %p and the comment you linked to. The compile-time checker doesn\u0027t give up on the entire format string if it sees a %p. It simply enforces that the corresponding argument passed is the size of a void pointer. The comment you link to talks about how a compile-time snprintf optimization pass wouldn\u0027t know the output size of the final string, since the output from %p is implementation defined. But that\u0027s not what we\u0027re turning on here.\n\nAs a heads up, %p is also in fact my plan for the next phases: %ph for our hex byte buffers and %pb for our binary int format. So it would make sense for %T too, the only reason I didn\u0027t do it is I thought people wouldn\u0027t like the NULL arguments that are required to go along with it. But it sounds like the CPRINT_TIMESTAMP() is worse, so maybe I should switch to it.",
      "parentUuid": "01238657_a4bdc783",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "0ab02441dc7eb1263fed95935d63bce7ad92e35a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
